    <section id="history">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-kicker">History &amp; significance</div>
        <h2>History and Significance of {ATTRACTION_FULL_NAME}</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-2">
          <div>
            <p>{ATTRACTION_SHORT_NAME} takes its name from the road that once ran from here towards Delhi. As one of the gates in Lahore’s Mughal city wall, it served as the eastern entrance, and the imperial road from Lahore Fort to Delhi formally began at this very threshold.</p>
            <p>Over the centuries the gate was repaired and rebuilt many times, yet its civic role endured: traders, craftspeople, travellers and visitors all passed through the same corridor. That is why Delhi Gate is described as a living gate rather than a closed monument.</p>
            <p>Today the Royal Trail restoration projects carried out by the Walled City of Lahore Authority (WCLA) have reopened a pedestrian route from Delhi Gate as far as {NEARBY_LANDMARK_1} and {NEARBY_LANDMARK_2}, turning the area into a centre for cultural tourism.</p>
            <h3>An architectural snapshot</h3>
            <p>Lahore’s Mughal wall was built of lime mortar and fired brick, and its gates were not mere openings but complete defensive and administrative units — a tall arched passage in the middle, guard and toll rooms on either side, light and air shafts above, and heavy timber leaves shut each night. This double identity of Delhi Gate — defensive gate and civic threshold — remains its defining feature.</p>
            <p>The sequence of gates in the walled city changed repeatedly: after the Mughal peak came repair and rebuilding under Sikh rule, and during the British period most of the wall was removed, yet a handful of gates — Delhi Gate among them — survived as landmarks in the city’s memory. Today’s Delhi Gate is therefore a layered historic document, where Mughal planning, later repairs and modern restoration can all be read at one spot.</p>
            <h3>A gate’s story and local traditions</h3>
            <p>Picture a caravan halting before this threshold at dawn centuries ago: for a traveller arriving from outside, the gate announced the city; for those leaving, it marked the start of the long road towards Delhi. Step inside and the Royal Trail began at once, where caravans, craftspeople and buyers merged into a single stream.</p>
            <p>Local tradition remembers Delhi Gate as the “edge” from which the inner city begins: the commerce of Akbari Mandi, the smell of spices, sacks of dry goods, the call to prayer from {NEARBY_LANDMARK_1} and the constant hum of the bazaar all gather around this one gate. These scenes are still alive today, which is why visitors say Delhi Gate is not a museum display but a piece of everyday life still running.</p>
            <div class="card">
              <h3>Historical periods at a glance</h3>
              <div class="facts">
                <div><span>Mughal era</span><strong>Founding of the walled city and the eastern gate on the imperial road towards Delhi</strong></div>
                <div><span>18th century</span><strong>Periods of conflict and repair; the gate keeps its defensive role</strong></div>
                <div><span>Sikh period</span><strong>Rebuilding, changes in paintwork and timber elements</strong></div>
                <div><span>British period</span><strong>Most of the wall removed; gates remain as city markers</strong></div>
                <div><span>Modern era</span><strong>WCLA restoration, pedestrian Royal Trail project and visitor facilities</strong></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Key facts</h3>
